Operational decisions in business processes increasingly start from unstructured input, such as complaints, e-mails or claims, that rule-based decision models cannot read. Language models can read such input, but their decision logic cannot be audited, and it is unclear when their output may be acted on without a human. We study System One models, which answer a fixed set of typed questions (choices, yes/no statements, ordinal scores) with a probability distribution per answer, and show how to turn such a model into an auditable decision service based on the Decision Model and Notation (DMN) standard. The model serves twice: as a teacher, whose holistic decisions on unlabelled cases are distilled into a verified DMN decision table over its own typed answers, and as a source of uncertainty, whose probabilities feed a confidence gate. The gate's threshold is chosen with Learn-then-Test, so that, with high probability, both the error rate of automated cases and the share of critical cases, such as frauds, cleared automatically stay below a chosen level. We evaluate the approach with Jev, a commercial System One model, on three decisions: monetary relief and queue routing for \res{data.sample} consumer complaints, and fraud screening for \res{jobfraud.cases} job postings. We report the fidelity and size of the distilled tables, the calibration of the model, the share of cases that can be automated at error levels of 1--10\%, the number of labelled critical cases such guarantees require, and the comparison with the model alone and with supervised baselines.
